        <body>
            <div xml:id="d99">

                <pb facs="0355" n="324" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76v31/pg324"/>

                <head>99. Memorandum From the Executive Secretary of the Economic Policy Board
                        (Porter) to President
                        Ford<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d99-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1975-08-29">August 29, 1975</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Economic Policy Board Executive Committee Vote on U.S. Negotiating
                        Position on Gold</item>
                </list>
                <p>As Executive Secretary of the Economic Policy Board, I was designated by
                    Secretary Simon and Mr. Seidman to conduct the secret ballot you
                    requested at this morning’s Economic and Energy Meeting<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d99-f2"/> on the issue of the U.S. negotiating position on gold.</p>
                <pb facs="0356" n="325" xml:id="pg325"/>
                <p>The closest refinement of the issue is as follows: <table cols="2" rows="2">
                        <row>
                            <cell>
                                <hi rendition="#italic">Treasury Position:</hi>
                            </cell>
                            <cell>Central banks should not be free to sell gold to one another for
                                the settlement of regular or normal transactions.</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>
                                <hi rendition="#italic">Federal Reserve Board Position:</hi>
                            </cell>
                            <cell>Central banks should not be free to sell gold to one another for
                                the settlement of regular or normal transactions <hi
                                    rendition="#italic">and</hi> central bank transactions in gold
                                must be restricted to emergency circumstances.</cell>
                        </row>
                    </table>
                </p>
                <p>The votes of the Executive Committee members are as follows: <table cols="2"
                        rows="3">
                        <row>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell>Treasury Position Supported by Simon, Dunlop,
                                Morton, Kissinger, Seidman<anchor n="3"
                                    xml:id="d99-f3"/>
                            </cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell>Federal Reserve Board Position Supported by Burns
                            </cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell>Abstentions: Lynn,
                                    Greenspan
                            </cell>
                        </row>
                    </table>
                </p>
                <p>The Department of State vote was cast by Deputy Secretary Ingersoll who spoke with Secretary Kissinger following the meeting.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d99-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d99-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Ford Library, President’s
                    Handwriting File, Subject File, Box 19, Finance—Gold. No classification marking.
                    Attached to an August 29 covering memorandum from Connor to President Ford
                    that reads: “Roger Porter sent this in
                    per your request.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d99-f2" n="2" xml:id="d99-f2-content">
                <p>The meeting took place in the Cabinet Room from 11:15 a.m. to 12:15 p.m. In
                    attendance were President Ford, Simon, Seidman, Dunlop, Lynn, Butz, Ingersoll, Domestic
                    Council Executive Director and President’s Assistant for Domestic Affairs James
                    Cannon III, Morton, Enders, Yeo, Domestic Council Deputy Director Richard
                    Dunham, Greenspan, Counselor John Marsh, Jr., Federal Energy Administration
                    Administrator Frank Zarb, Counselor Robert
                        Hartmann, Cheney, Rumsfeld, President’s Assistant for Legislative
                    Affairs Max Friedersdorf, Press Secretary Ronald Nessen, Assistant Press
                    Secretary John Carlson, and Porter.
                    (Ibid., President’s Daily Diary) No other record of the meeting has been
                    found.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d99-f3" n="3" xml:id="d99-f3-content">
                <p>President Ford initialed his approval of
                    this position. Seidman, Simon, Porter, and Rumsfeld were
                    apprised of the President’s decision that day, according to an August 29
                    memorandum from Connor to Seidman. (Ibid., President’s Handwriting File,
                    Subject File, Box 19, Finance—Gold)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
